# Response headers for Cloudflare Pages and Netlify.
#
# This is the annotated source of truth for the hardening headers. vercel.json
# must express EXACTLY the same policy — JSON has no comments, so
# the reasoning lives here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Permissions-Policy: deny everything, then hand back the two things we use.
#
# The app needs a Bluetooth radio and a screen that stays awake. It has no
# business asking for anything else, so every other powerful feature is denied
# outright with an empty allowlist `()` — not merely unused, but unavailable, so
# a future dependency-free-but-careless edit cannot quietly start using one.
#
#   bluetooth=(self)          Web Bluetooth. The whole point of the app.
#   screen-wake-lock=(self)   The automation runner holds a wake lock so a long
#                             image queue is not killed by the screen locking.
#
# Both already default to `self`; naming them documents the intent and keeps the
# deny-list honest. Browsers that do not know a feature name ignore that entry
# (Chrome logs a console notice) — harmless, and worth it for the ones that do.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Content-Security-Policy notes:
#
# connect-src is 'self', NOT 'none'. The app itself never makes a network request
# at runtime, so 'none' looks right — and the two things people expect it to
# break, it does not break:
#
#   * Service worker REGISTRATION is unaffected. Fetching sw.js is a worker
#     request, matched by worker-src (CSP3 falls worker-src back to child-src,
#     then script-src, then default-src). connect-src is never consulted.
#   * The MANIFEST fetch is unaffected. <link rel="manifest"> is matched by
#     manifest-src, falling back to default-src. Again not connect-src.
#
# What 'none' does break is everything the worker does after that. A worker runs
# under the CSP delivered with its own script response — this policy, since the
# rule below applies to every path — and the requests it makes are connect-src
# requests: plain fetch(), and cache.add()/addAll(), which are defined in terms
# of fetch with an empty request destination.
#
# Under 'none' the worker would therefore install, activate, and never fetch a
# byte. Nothing is precached; and since sw.js is cache-first, every cache miss
# it then handles has no fallback left. Registering successfully makes that
# worse, not better — the app breaks *more* thoroughly than if the worker had
# failed outright, and it does it silently.
#
# 'self' is the narrowest value that actually works. It still blocks every
# cross-origin request, so there is no path off this origin for a photo picked
# out of the library or anything read back from the display. Web Bluetooth is not
# governed by CSP at all, so nothing here constrains the device link.
#
# To check any of this for yourself in 30 seconds: change connect-src in
# serve.js's SECURITY_HEADERS, hard-reload, and watch DevTools -> Application ->
# Service Workers alongside the console. Under 'none' you should see the worker
# register and activate normally, "Refused to connect … connect-src" errors
# raised from sw.js, and Application -> Cache Storage left empty.
#
# script-src 'self' forbids inline <script> — entry points must be
# <script type="module" src="./js/main.js">. style-src 'self' likewise forbids
# both <style> blocks and inline style="..." attributes; all CSS belongs in
# css/app.css. Setting element.style.* from JS is CSSOM, not CSP, and is fine.
#
# img-src allows data: and blob: because the preview canvas and the file picker
# produce exactly those.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), ambient-light-sensor=(), autoplay=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), hid=(), idle-detection=(), local-fonts=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), serial=(), usb=(), bluetooth=(self), screen-wake-lock=(self)
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# The files that decide whether an update is ever seen. Everything else reaches
# the browser through the service worker and is versioned by its CACHE_VERSION,
# but these are fetched by the browser itself and must not sit in an HTTP cache.
# sw.js is the critical one: the worker is only replaced when the browser sees
# that its bytes changed, so an HTTP-cached sw.js is a worker that can never be
# updated — and with a cache-first worker, that freezes the whole app.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/app.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache
